Bayara's topography is dominated by the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Western Plains]] which takes up roughly 70% of the community's total territory. The relatively flat coast dominates in terms of population and economic resources, and is the cultural and political center from which Bayara expanded following the [[Colonial Balisca|Illypnian colonization of Balisca]]. Because of the expansive plains, Bayara is commonly known as the {{W|breadbasket}} of Balisca. The coast is formed by the bays of [[Terranexo]], [[Serpiedra]], and [[Selvadia Bay|Selvadia]]. In the south there are prominent {{W|slope landform|slope}}s near the sea, which also feature diverse environments, such as {{W|Atlantic Coast restingas|restinga vegetation}}, {{W|bay}}s, {{W|lagoon}}s and {{W|tropical forest}}s. 

Prior to Illypnian arrival, what is now Bayara was sparsely populated by several indigenous groups, most notably the of the [[Kurupiranga|Kurupiranga peoples]] (including the [[Kuarah'ka]], the Kurupiran-Nyengatú and the [[Caiçara]]). Illypnian exploration began with its discovery by [[Crown of Volisania|Volisan explorer]] [[Raimon de Montcada i Cardona]], who landed at [[Bahia de las Palmas]] on June 2, 1500. Following the issuing of the [[Abyala Charter]], it was disputed between Illypnian expeditions in what became known as the [[Reapers' War]], from which the Xacano emerged victorious. Bayara was [[Battle of Navassa|the last]] of the republics to join the [[Northern Confederation]], and was the main theatre of action during the [[Baliscan War of Independence|last years of the ''Resistencía'']]. Bayara would declare it's secession from the Confederation in 1828, sparking the [[Baliscan Civil War]]. The Bayaran secessionists were ultimately defeated by 1838 and reintegrated into the confederation.

Today, Bayara is distinctive for its large [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous]] and {{W|expatriate}} communities, high population growth, as well as for its increasing {{W|Climate change|environmental challenges}}. The nation’s economy relies mainly on {{W|Tourism industry|tourism}}, {{W|agriculture industry|agriculture}}, construction and transportation, which developed later than the rest of Balisca in the late 20th century. Bayara is also renowned for its agricultural products, including sugar cane and coffee, its beautiful landscapes, the touristy "[[Solana|Bayaran Riviera]]", and as a popular destination for {{W|Expatriate|retirees}}. Bayara is comprised of some of the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|flattest regions]] of Balisca, with most of the country holding 75% of the {{W|fertile land}} within the Confederation. 

==Etymology==
The name ''Bayara'' is derived from the [[w:Andalusi Arabic|Andalusi Bayani]] word ''al-bayāra'' ({{Langx|ar|البيارة}}), meaning "the [[W:Orchard|orchard]]" or "the [[W:Plantation|plantation]]" (specifically referring to a cultivated grove). This toponym serves as a linguistic fossil of the region's unique colonial demography. Following the [[W:War of the Alpujarras|War of the Alpujarras]] (1568–1571) and the [[W:Expulsion of the Moriscos|Expulsion of the Moriscos]] (1609), thousands of [[W:Moriscos|Moriscos]] (Christianized Muslims) and Andalusians were forcibly resettled in the territory by the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]]. These settlers applied the Bayani term to the fertile, Mesogean-like coastal plains where they established the archipelago's first large-scale agricultural estates.

The name proved prophetic, as Bayara subsequently developed into the center of the colonial [[W:Plantation economy|plantation economy]], dominating the production of [[W:Sugar|sugar]], [[W:Brazilwood|brazilwood]], and [[W:Coffee|coffee]]. While the name was adapted into [[Xacano language|Jacano]] orthography (often appearing as ''A Baiara'' in early colonial manuscripts, reflecting the definite article), the phonetic structure remained largely unchanged from its Bayani root. This distinguishes Bayara from other Baliscan regions, whose names are typically derived from the [[W:Tupi-Guarani languages|Kurupiran-Nyengatú languages]] ([[Araucaria]]), [[Ajami]], [[W:Catalan language|Volisanian]] (e.g., [[Cerdanya]]), or Catholic liturgy (e.g., [[Sant Chuan]]).

==History==
===Pre-European arrival===
===European exploration===
====Spanish colonization====
{{Main|Reapers' War}}
[[File:Palmares massacre.png|left|thumb|The [[Palmares revolt|Palmares uprising]] and subsequent massacre of former slaveowners marked the beginning of the [[Maroon Wars]]]]
As early as 1537, Illypnians conquistadors chartered by the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Crown of Volisania]] extensively explored the coastal region of what is now Bayara, establishing extensive claims motivated by their desire to exploit of the abundant [[W:Brasilwood|Brasilwood]] and other natural resources, versus the Aragonese endeavors which were facilitating the resettlement of persecuted ethno-religious groups (such as [[Catharism|Cathars]], [[W:Sephardic Jews|Sephardic Jews]], [[Mozbayana|Mozbayán]] and {{W|Mudejar}}s from the the Iberian Peninsula and the {{W|Maghreb}} to Balisca. The colony of [[Macaio]] was established on 11 January 1538, and was quickly followed by Salvador, Macao, and [[Uxiu]] as they explored up the [[Tapajós River]].

In 1521 Spanish colonial authorities, relying heavily on Portuguese slave traffickers, passed a series of ordinances to limit the number of Muslim Kaftians taken to their colonies in the Algarves. Many of these Africans were captives of war with military experience, and they were viewed as particularly dangerous. The Aragonese colonies' desire for slave labor outweighed their fears, which were well-founded as enslaved Africans (Muslims and non-Muslims alike) continued to rebel. Over the next three centuries there would continue to be violent instances of African revolts in the colonies of Bayara, preventing their settlement expansion further into the interior from the coast. 

In Spain, discontent amongst the the {{W|Moriscos}} under the jurisdiction of the {{W|Crown of Castile}} led to a rebellion 1568. The {{W|War of the Alpujarras}} (as it came to be called), was fought until 1571, when the Aragonese Crown intervened, enforcing the [[W:Peace of Granada|Peace of Granada]] that same year. By the rebellion’s end, a significant proportion of Castile's formerly Muslim population was expelled, dispersing across the [[W:Spanish Empire|Spanish Empire]], primarily across the Atlantic to Balisca. Sizable numbers also relocated from [[Marrascania]] in North Africa, out of fear of potential future persecution by the Castilian council.

The Peace of Granada had significant implications for the development of colonization in Balisca, as it codified their rights to property within the Spanish Empire, and established the Council of [[Mozarabia]] as an ''{{W|aeque principaliter}}'' state of Spain, representative of the the Moriscos and [[Mozarabia|Mozarabs]]. However tensions remained high in Spain, and instead of expelling the Moriscos or compelling them to abandon the last vestiges of their Arabic culture, the ''Cortes'' proposed a resettlement program: any Mozarab household that relinquished its lands in Spain would receive lands of three times the acreage in Balisca (primarily in the territory of Bayara) or a stately home and a 5-year annual stipend of 24,000 reales in any Baliscan land under Aragonese patronage. The sudden influx of [[Mozarabic]]-speaking Christians and Muslims also led to small scale conversions among the native population that remained amongst them.

Relative to other European colonies, particularly those of the Spanish, the footholds on the Bayaran coast were considered exceptionally "poor and dangerous" place. In 1609, King [[W:Philip III of Spain|Philip III of Spain]] decreed the [[W:Expulsion of the Moriscos|Expulsion of the Moriscos]], resulting in the dispersal of over three million Moriscos across the the Spanish Empire. The Castilian council forcibly moved many Moriscos to Balisca in an attempt bolster their claims to the archipelago.

=== Independence movements and revolution ===
{{Main|United Provinces of Atlántida|Espanya Boba}}

Angered by what he saw as [[W:Sedition|sedition]] by the [[Crown of Aragon|realms of the Crown of Aragon]] (including Bayara and Cerdanya) which had supported the claim of [[W:Charles VI, Holy Roman Emperor|Charles of Austria]] to the Spanish thrones during the [[W:War of Spanish Succession|War of Spanish Succession]] and taking his native [[W:Kingdom of France|France]] as a model of a centralized state, [[W:Philip V of Spain|Philip V]] suppressed the institutions, privileges, and the ancient [[W:Fuero|charters]] of almost all the areas that were formerly part of the Crown of Aragon ([[W:Kingdom of Aragon|Kingdom of Aragon]], [[W:Principality of Catalonia|Principality of Catalonia]], [[W:Kingdom of Valencia|Kingdom of Valencia]], and the [[W:Kingdom of Majorca|Kingdom of Majorca]]). The decrees ruled that all the territories in the Crown of Aragon except the [[W:Aran Valley|Aran Valley]] were to be ruled by the laws of [[W:Crown of Castile|Castile]] ("the most praiseworthy in all the Universe" according to the 1707 decree), embedding those regions into a new and nearly uniformly administered, centralised [[W:Spain|Spain]].

This event greatly impacted the governance and threatened the relative autonomy that Balisca had under the jurisdiction of the Crown of Aragon. Additionally, the population, at this time composed mainly of [[Catharism|Cathars]], [[W:Sephardic Jews|Sephardic Jews]], [[W:Muladí|Muslims]], [[W:Mozarabs|Mozarabs]], and [[W:Mudéjar|Mudéjar]]<nowiki/>s, staunchly opposed the centralization of power imposed by the Nueva Planta decrees. Having experienced persecution against their respective communities, they adamantly rejected the authority of the now-dominant Castilian Catholics. Their resistance was fueled by a desire to preserve their distinct cultural identities and religious practices.

Amidst this resistance, Baliscan colonists faced a challenging dilemma—whether to bow to the centralizing authority of the new Spanish government or to pursue their struggle for autonomy and cultural preservation. The clash of these ideologies shaped the socio-political landscape of Balisca and influenced its trajectory within the Spanish realm.

[[W:Spain under Joseph Bonaparte|Spain under Joseph Bonaparte]] attempted to forcibly re-impose colonial rule in the Baliscan archipelago, marking the end of the [[Espanya Boba|"Espanya Boba"]] period of Baliscan history stretching back to the early 18th century. Although there has been research on the idea of a pan-Baliscan identity that overlapped the early political boundaries, political unification of the archipelago was not the aim of most, nor was it necessarily inevitable. In fact, there was little coordination between the different factions in the opening years, despite their mutual interest in resisting the Spanish attempt at conquest.
[[File:Battle of Navassa.png|left|thumb|Defeat of the Royalist garrison at the [[Battle of Navassa]]]]
The Volisans had wider support in the economic Bayaran economic elite, in the hopes of preserving slavery (upon which it's profitable plantation economy was based). This support intensified to the [[Proclamation of Alcazaba]], in which the [[Northern Confederation]] pledged to "fully eradicate" the practice. However beyond this, the Bayarans had little affection for the new Bonapartist regime in Spain and it's struggle for legitimacy.

In 1815, representatives of Royalist Bayara surrendered to the confederal forces following the decisive [[Battle of Navassa]], effectively destroying the last royalist strongholds in mainland Balisca, allowing the rebels to consolidate ending the threat of invasion against the Confederation. While this did not change the position of Spain against separatism, it resulted in the collapse of royalists forces Balisca. Over the course of the next two years, the Anti-royalist armies won major victories and consolidated their control in their respective provinces. The continued political instability in Spain, without a navy, army or treasury, convinced many former royalists of the need to formally abandon their cause and embrace independence.

===Post-independence===
{{See also|Pastry War|Confederal Question}}
[[File:Zouave - Grolleron crop.jpg|thumb|298x298px|Cerdanyan ''Zuau'' in the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]], <abbr>c.</abbr> 1825]]
Independence also did not result in the end of political turmoil, and was immediately followed by inter-republic conflict. During the war, confederal forces had imprisoned, hanged, or expelled all former slave owners (most relocated to [[Sanrobonia]]), and divided their land and other assets between the newly emancipated Kaftian population. This further hardened attitudes in Bayara against confederation, which already was strong due to the devastation in Bayara in terms of infrastructure and the economy. Because of the reluctance of the land-owning class accept the loss of their privileges and to grant voting rights to freedmen, the Confederal Congress adopted [[Reconstruction laws]] over Bayaran objections. In response, and intending to avoid the social effects of the war, most of the Bayaran provinces passed [[W:Black codes|black codes]]. In the early years of confederation, these were mostly ''de jure'' nullified by confederal law and pro-reformist legislatures, but widely continued to be enforced by Bayaran officials.

Increasing calls for the strengthening of confederal power and increasing Hesperian influence in the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]] fueled intercommunal tensions between predominately Volisanian-speaking Cathars and Xacano-speaking Catholics, and the confederal government was increasingly dragged into the position of mediator in the region. Conflict boiled over following the murder of an Volisanian [[W:Pastry|pastry]] chef in [[Xàbia]] by a Catholic mob, resulting in [[Pastry War|escalating reprisals and counter-reprisals]] which eventually culminated in [[Confederal emergency|confederal intervention]] and the raising of the [[Cerdanyan Legion]] as a peacekeeping force to administer the Estuarian regions. It was ultimately ended by the [[Treaty of Navassa]]. The Treaty was largely viewed as illegitimate by Bayarans (who denounced it as unacceptably favorable to the Catalans) and Estuarians (who were frustrated by it's failure to secure their rights) alike, and became increasingly hard to enforce. It established military districts and governors to rule over the region until new governments could be established. Many Xacano landowners who had actively supported the royalists were temporarily disenfranchised. The Legion was withdrawn in 1826 over confederal disputes in regards to the legion's funding.

====Civil war and reconstruction====
{{Main|Baliscan Civil War|Anyos Federales}}

Hesperian delegates called for a [[Confederal Convention]] to solve the impending crisis, to be held in [[Alessandria]]. The convention adopted the [[Compromise of 1827]], providing for a significantly strengthened central government to handle disputes between the republics and their citizens. This was unanimously supported by the delegations of Hesperia and [[Cerdanya]], but was rejected and ultimately ignored by the Bayarans, with the exception of the delegation from [[Macaio]]. These delegates were at the time aware that the republic was well on it's way towards renouncing the [[Articles of Confederation]] and {{W|secession|seceding}} from the union, and sought to pre-empt this by declaring the [[Carpinchera Republic]]. The General Junta announced it's denunciation of Articles of Confederation on 6 July 1828, and the [[Xunta Nacionalista]] assumed provisional powers in anticipation of war.
[[File:Capture_of_Baxuran_guerrillas.png|right|thumb|300x300px|Pro-secessionist guerrilla fighters captured in [[Aigües]] during the [[Eivissa|Estuarian wetlands]] campaign]]
Despite controlling a numerical troop advantage throughout the war, the Bayarans were forced to divert significant numbers of troops towards their efforts to subdue the intensifying uprisings and ambushes by Maroons and [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous peoples]] resisting the Xunta's policy of [[Maroon resettlement|forced removal]] from the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Western Plains]]. Secessionist forces would go on to wage of violent campaign of expulsion, in the process successfully destroying dozens of ''quilombos'' and expelling their inhabitants to the territory of the [[Maroon Confederacy]]. Notably however, Bayaran armies crossed the [[Cerroitá Pass]] and attempted to capture the [[Alpujarra]], culminating in the [[Siege of Castejón]], which was beaten back with the intervention of the [[Maroon Confederacy]] in support of pro-confederation forces. By 1835, most Bayaran pro-secessionist land and naval forces had either disbanded, surrendered or otherwise ceased hostilities.

By 1835, most Bayaran pro-secessionist land and naval forces had either disbanded, surrendered or otherwise ceased hostilities. Despite this the war lacked a formal end, with bands of secessionist guerrilla forces fighting, surrendering or disbanding sporadically throughout most of 1835-1837. Tensions reignited after a massacre in [[Brumavent]], in which pro-secessionist soldiers executed the hundreds of captives and vowed to continue their guerrilla warfare. This triggered significant unrest on both sides and reignited the already high tensions between the Catalans and Aragonese that resided in the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Western Plains]]. Reprisals and counter reprisals escalated, and the two factions subsequently fought a series of battles across the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]], from which the pro-confederation forces again emerged victorious. Bayara suffered substantially more than the rest of the confederation overall, as the federalist strategy of [[W:Attrition warfare|attrition warfare]] meant that Bayaran forces could not replace their casualties, and the total war waged by Hesperian armies devastated the infrastructure and caused widespread poverty and distress.

The seceding provinces were punished severely in the [[Treaty of Bixessarri]]: they were forced to pay the expenses and debts incurred by the Confederation during the war, in addition to the the Legislative Assembly's being coerced into ratifying the Compromise, resulting what became known as the [[Ani Federali|Reconstruction era]]. Communes and regions within Bayara that had sided with the confederalists following the [[Brumavent massacre]] largely avoided occupation. In addition, the Confederal Council forced the {{W|cede|ceding}} of significant amounts of land in Bayara's east, including [[Trás-as-Montanyas]] (ceded to [[Hesperia]]), and the southern wetlands, which was allocated to [[Estuaria|Estuarian community]]. 

=== Industrialization'', Comuncazo'' and political dissolution ===
{{Main|Baliscan Revolution}}

Bayara's industrial development, from 1840 into the 1930s, was gradual and inward looking. Initially, industry was closely associated with agriculture: cotton plantations led to the growth of [[W:Textile manufacturing|textile manufacturing]]. Coffee planters were among the early industrial investors. However, this rapid, export-driven growth led to increasing societal stratification. The [[Volisan aristocracy]] wielded disproportionate economic and political power at the republican and confederal level, causing simmering unrest amongst the Bayaran populace. Restrictions on imports forced by the First World War and government policies of "import substitution" and trade tariffs, all contributed to industrial growth.

The boom in immigration provided a market for goods, and sectors such as food processing grew. However the rapidly growing urban population grew increasingly resentful of the economic elite, which had combined its economic power with the electoral strength of immigrant communities and the employing of [[W:Machine politics|machine politics]] to maintain their dominant position. The stand-off was also political: politics had been long monopolized by the [[Bayaran Republican Party]] (PRB), but by the eve of the Baliscan Revolution, left-wing ([[Anarchism in Balisca|predominately anarchist]]) movements had been growing in opposition and the country was increasingly poised for violent unrest. The working conditions and the oligarchic political system of the industrialization era, which made it difficult for workers to participate, meant that anarchism quickly gained strength among workers. [[W:Revolutionary syndicalism|Revolutionary syndicalism]] exerted a great influence on the workers' movement, especially at workers' congresses and in the strikes of the period. Anarchists also contributed to the creation of a series of periodicals for the workers' press and founded several Modern Schools around the country.

The prolongation of the war in Europe helped to bring about, in 1916, an explosion of industrial production. As imported products stopped reaching Bayaran ports, national industries returned to employing workers to meet growing demand. However, there was a considerable increase in the cost of living, given that several products were exported to countries in conflict. In 1916, the prices of rice, sugar and corn increased significantly; the following year, the wholesale prices of various products went up, such as beans and [[W:Cassava|manioc flour]]; and wheat became scarce and expensive. Wages, however, remained at the same levels as in the years before the war. Food prices continued to rise during the first six months of 1917. Basic items tended to be 20% to 150% more expensive than the previous year. The favorable environment for the satisfaction of demands due to the resumption of industrial activity led the labor movement to an accelerated process of reorganization in disarticulated resistance societies during the crisis, resulting in a strike wave starting in 1917.

In 1917, the PRB attempted to amend the republic's constitution in order to reinforce the existing socio-economic dynamic and thwart the growing radicalism. 
[[File:Revolução de 1930 (18).jpg|thumb|Bayaran strikers in Macaio celebrating the coup against the Confederal Congress during the Baliscan Revolution]]
The contentious [[1918 Cerdanyan general election]] saw the victory of Pol Pérez, a [[Progress Party (Cerdanya)|notorious crony capitalist]]. His election, widely condemned as fraudulent, sparked outrage across the confederation. Left-wing activists demanded the overthrow of the aristocracy and a re-run of the election, a call that quickly spread across the nation. On 28 October 1918, over four million students, laborers, and union members took to the streets in what would become known as the [[1918 Baliscan general strike]]. 

The movement escalated on 21 April 1919, with the [[storming of the Baliscan Cabildo]] by rebelling soldiers supported by civilians in reaction to increasingly violent repression by the state. The successful coup against the Confederal Congress and the seizure the Cabildo [[W:Power of the deed|instigated]] the second, more violent phase of the Baliscan Revolution, ultimately leading to a significant rise in {{W|political violence}} nationwide. Tensions further escalated with the assassination of Deputy Premier [[Moisès Vilchez]] by [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist]] [[Miquel Cebrián]]. With the country on the verge of a violent {{W|civil war}}, the Council of Ministers narrowly voted against {{W|vote of no confidence|confidence}} in Premier [[Máximo Burjassot]], forcing the administration to dissolve. Faced with continued unrest on the streets and an increasingly demoralized and hostile military, the Confederal Council called a [[1919 Confederal Convention|Confederal Convention]].

The political revolution that followed was transformative. It resulted in the abolition of the confederal republican system and implemented {{W|anarchism|anarchist}} and {{W|Libertarian socialism|libertarian socialist}} principles. Internal borders were abolished, secularization was enacted, and {{W|Socialist self-management}} took root. Araucaria’s economy, like much of Balisca’s, was transformed with significant portions placed under {{W|worker control}}. Agrarian areas were collectivized and operated as libertarian socialist communes, while social welfare programs were expanded through income and {{W|wealth redistribution}}. Socioeconomic policies were heavily influenced by the {{W|Italian Futurism|Italian}} and {{W|Russian Futurism|Russian Futurist}} movements, eventually coalescing into an [[Baliscan Accelerationism|accelerationist]] ideology. This period also accelerated the process of {{W|indigenous decolonization}} that had begun with the [[1883 Confederal Convention]]. 

